1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,683
Outlook was down for about a day.

2
00:00:02,683 --> 00:00:04,600
And it wasn't a hack.

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:06,184
On November 12,

4
00:00:06,184 --> 00:00:08,551
Microsoft's email servers started failing their

5
00:00:08,551 --> 00:00:10,919
own safety checks and dropped offline.

6
00:00:10,919 --> 00:00:13,681
The rest got overloaded and failed too.

7
00:00:13,681 --> 00:00:15,259
Outlook and Teams went

8
00:00:15,259 --> 00:00:16,838
down around the world.

9
00:00:16,838 --> 00:00:19,600
Microsoft hasn't published the full cause yet.

10
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:21,289
Next time,

11
00:00:21,289 --> 00:00:24,511
check Microsoft's status page first.

12
00:00:24,511 --> 00:00:26,444
Expect late emails.

13
00:00:26,444 --> 00:00:29,022
Move anything urgent to

14
00:00:29,022 --> 00:00:31,600
a call or WhatsApp.

15
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,417
Should you switch to Google?

16
00:00:34,417 --> 00:00:35,383
You can,

17
00:00:35,383 --> 00:00:37,800
but Google goes down too,

18
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:40,700
and moving a company is expensive.

19
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:43,600
A backup plan costs far less.

20
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,544
You can't stop Microsoft from breaking,

21
00:00:46,544 --> 00:00:49,512
but you can keep your business running.

22
00:00:49,512 --> 00:00:51,208
Pick a second channel,

23
00:00:51,208 --> 00:00:52,904
keep key contacts offline,

24
00:00:52,904 --> 00:00:54,600
and practise it once.

25
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,556
What did your office do

26
00:00:57,556 --> 00:00:59,600
when Outlook went down?
